    <template v-if="activeModal === 'rommel'"><p class="eyebrow">Rommel's testimony</p><h2 id="rommel-title">Saved by grace</h2><p>I grew up in a Catholic family. At a young age, I simply followed the religion of my parents. I served as an altar boy and was active in the Catholic youth group. I even participated in religious ceremonies, including carrying a cross in an Easter procession, believing that these religious activities could somehow help me get to heaven.</p><p>My eldest sister was the first in our family to be saved. I attended Baptist church services and heard the Gospel while I was in high school, but I never truly understood what it meant to be saved.</p><p>During college and the years that followed, I developed sinful habits and a worldly lifestyle, but after losing my father, God began to soften my heart and draw me to Himself.</p><p>One day, God used a daily devotional about salvation to show me my true spiritual condition. I realized that I was a sinner and that my religion, good works, and religious activities could never save me. I needed Jesus Christ.</p><p>In 2004, one morning while sitting at my desk in the office, I bowed my head and asked God to save me. I placed my faith in Jesus Christ and trusted Him alone for my salvation.</p><p>Not long afterward, I brought my mother and siblings to Alpha Baptist Church. God continued to work in their hearts, and eventually every one of them was saved. Our family was baptized together.</p><p><em>“For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God.” Ephesians 2:8</em></p></template>
    <template v-else-if="activeModal === 'shiela'"><p class="eyebrow">Shiela's testimony</p><h2 id="shiela-title">A faithful invitation</h2><p>I am thankful for a coworker who eventually became one of my good friends. She faithfully invited me to church many times, but because I grew up in a Catholic background, I always found reasons not to go.</p><p>One day, she invited me to attend a youth camp. As a young lady who loved the outdoors, I was finally convinced to go.</p><p>I still remember the preaching at that camp. The message was about hell, and it caused me to seriously consider where I would spend eternity. A lady sat down with me and showed me from the Bible how I could be saved. In June of 1998, I accepted Jesus Christ as my personal Saviour.</p><p>Although I had trusted Christ, I did not immediately grow in my faith. Then, in 2004, God helped me gain assurance of my salvation. I followed the Lord in baptism and began faithfully attending a Bible-believing Baptist church, where I started growing in my relationship with the Lord.</p><p>I am especially thankful that God used the persistence and faithfulness of a friend who never stopped inviting me to church.</p></template>
    <template v-else-if="activeModal === 'ryle'"><p class="eyebrow">Ryle's testimony</p><h2 id="ryle-title">Assurance in Christ</h2><p>When I was six years old, I remember hearing about salvation, but I never really knew what it meant. Coming home from church one night, I asked my dad what it meant to be saved. He took me through the Gospel and I made a profession of faith that day.</p><p>Coming to Northwest Bible Baptist Church was a big change for me. I had never felt conviction in my life before, so I doubted my salvation. All the way up until 2022, I felt fear and insecurity about where I would go when I died.</p><p>At football camp in August of 2022, Bro. Nichols preached a powerful message on hell. It was not until Thursday night of that week that God moved in my heart and I went forward for assurance. Bro. Jeremy Huston dealt with me, I got my assurance, and I have never doubted since that day.</p></template>
    <template v-else><p class="eyebrow">God's call on our lives</p><h2 id="calling-title">The full story</h2><p>In 2005, I enrolled in Bible college but was unable to finish my first semester when God opened the door for me to move to the United States. In 2006, Shiela and I got married. For the next thirteen years, we were members of a Baptist church in Schaumburg, Illinois, where I taught a Filipino Sunday School class and served as a deacon.</p><p>In 2019, my family and I began attending Northwest Bible Baptist Church. In the following year, while attending the Providence Baptist College graduation, God renewed my burden for ministry. When Dr. Keith Gomez said, “There is something about finishing what you started,” those words deeply convicted me.</p><p>Over the next few years, God continued preparing my heart. We faithfully participated in soul winning, discipleship, and other ministries in our church. Through missions conferences, conversations with missionaries, and summer trips to the Philippines, my desire for ministry became stronger.</p><p>With my pastor's encouragement and God's provision, I enrolled in Providence Baptist College in January 2025 to pursue a Master's degree in Biblical Studies. I began teaching a Tagalog Sunday School class and preaching regularly in our nursing home ministry.</p><p>As I balanced work, school, family, and ministry, I began earnestly praying that God would clearly show me His will concerning full-time ministry.</p><p>For twenty-five years, I had built a good career and enjoyed financial security. In January 2026, God brought the story of Abraham in Genesis 12 to my heart. I came to realize that following God's will would require me to trust Him beyond what was comfortable and secure.</p><p>That same month, my company announced a departmental restructuring. Because I had already expressed my desire to leave and pursue missions, I volunteered to be included in the workforce reduction. I had a deep sense of peace, knowing that God had answered my prayer and opened the door for me to follow His will. On January 21, 2026, my family and I surrendered to full-time missions.</p></template>
